            "\n",
            "--- Discussion ---\n",
            "The pre-trained PerceptronTagger often outperforms a simple HMM due to several factors:\n",
            "1. Training Data Size & Diversity: Pre-trained models are typically trained on much larger and more diverse corpora than our Brown corpus subset.\n",
            "2. Feature Engineering: Perceptron models can incorporate a richer set of features (e.g., word prefixes/suffixes, surrounding words, capitalization) beyond simple bigram transitions and emissions.\n",
            "3. Handling Unknown Words: While our HMM uses a basic '_UNKNOWN_' token, pre-trained models often have more sophisticated mechanisms for handling OOV words or words not seen in training.\n",
            "4. Model Complexity: Perceptrons, being discriminative models, can capture complex relationships between features and tags more effectively than generative HMMs, especially for fine-grained distinctions.\n",
            "This comparison highlights the trade-offs between implementing a model from scratch for understanding and using robust, feature-rich pre-trained models for higher performance.\n"
